-- Starter articles. They describe the exam pattern and general preparation method only; edit or replace them in Admin > Blog.
insert into blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, tags, status, published_at) values
('ssc-cgl-tier-1-exam-pattern',
 'SSC CGL Tier I exam pattern: sections, marks and timing',
 'A clear breakdown of the SSC CGL Tier I paper: four sections, 100 questions, 200 marks, 60 minutes and negative marking, and what it means for how you attempt it.',
 E'The SSC CGL Tier I is an objective, computer-based test. Knowing its structure before you start practising is the fastest way to stop wasting marks.\n\n## The paper at a glance\n\n- **Sections:** General Intelligence and Reasoning, General Awareness, Quantitative Aptitude, English Comprehension\n- **Questions:** 25 per section, 100 in total\n- **Marks:** 2 per question, 200 in total\n- **Time:** 60 minutes for the whole paper\n- **Negative marking:** 0.5 mark deducted for each wrong answer\n\nAlways confirm the details in the latest official notification, because the commission can change the pattern.\n\n## What the pattern means for you\n\n1. **Time is the real constraint.** Sixty minutes for 100 questions is about 36 seconds a question. Mock tests are how you learn to move on.\n2. **Negative marking punishes guessing.** A wrong answer costs a quarter of what a right answer earns, so guess only when you can remove at least one option.\n3. **Sections are equal in marks.** Do not spend 25 minutes on one section because it feels easy.\n\n## A simple way to use this\n\nTake a full-length mock under the real time limit, then look at section-wise time and accuracy. The section where you lose the most marks per minute is where your next week of practice should go.\n\nYou can try the pattern in a real exam-style window with the [free sample test](/tests).',
 array['SSC CGL','Exam pattern'], 'published', now()),
('how-to-analyse-a-mock-test',
 'How to analyse a mock test so your score actually improves',
 'Taking more mocks is not the same as improving. Use this four-step review after every test to find the marks you are losing and fix them.',
 E'Many aspirants take a mock, look at the score and move on. The score is the least useful part of the result. The review is where the improvement happens.\n\n## Step 1: Sort every mistake\n\nPut each wrong or skipped question in one of four groups:\n\n- **Did not know the concept**\n- **Knew it but made a calculation or reading error**\n- **Ran out of time**\n- **Guessed and got it wrong**\n\nThe group tells you what to fix. A concept gap needs study. A careless error needs a slower, cleaner method. A time problem needs a different order of attempting.\n\n## Step 2: Check accuracy by section and topic\n\nA section score hides detail. Look at accuracy by topic and find the two or three topics that cost you the most. Practise those before taking the next full mock.\n\n## Step 3: Read the solution even for questions you got right\n\nA correct answer reached the long way costs time. The solution often shows a faster route you can reuse.\n\n## Step 4: Write down one change for the next test\n\nOne specific change is enough: attempt reasoning first, skip any question that takes more than a minute on the first pass, or revise percentage shortcuts. Check next time whether it worked.\n\nEvery test on Sarkari Match shows section-wise and topic-wise analysis with a solution for each question, so you can follow these steps straight from your result page.',
 array['Mock tests','Strategy'], 'published', now() - interval '1 day'),
('ssc-cgl-preparation-strategy-for-beginners',
 'SSC CGL preparation strategy for beginners',
 'A practical order of work for first-time SSC CGL aspirants: learn the pattern, build basics, practise by topic, then move to full-length mocks.',
 E'If you are starting SSC CGL preparation, the hardest part is knowing what to do first. This order works for most beginners.\n\n## 1. Learn the exam before you study\n\nRead the latest notification and understand the sections, marks and timing. Then take one mock test cold. It will feel hard, and that is the point: it shows you your starting point.\n\n## 2. Build basics section by section\n\n- **Quantitative Aptitude:** arithmetic first (percentages, ratio, profit and loss, time and work), then algebra and geometry.\n- **Reasoning:** series, analogies, coding-decoding and syllogisms are quick to improve with practice.\n- **English:** vocabulary and grammar rules in short daily sessions, plus reading practice for comprehension.\n- **General Awareness:** a little every day beats long sessions before the exam.\n\n## 3. Practise by topic\n\nAfter learning a topic, solve a set of questions on it straight away and read every solution. Aim for accuracy first and speed second.\n\n## 4. Move to full-length mocks\n\nOnce your topics are in shape, take full mocks under the real 60-minute limit, ideally a few a week, and review each one properly (see our guide on [how to analyse a mock test](/blog/how-to-analyse-a-mock-test)).\n\n## 5. Keep a weekly rhythm\n\nPlan each week: topic practice on most days, one or two mocks, and one review day. Adjust the plan each week from your results rather than from how you feel.\n\nStart with the [free sample test](/tests) to see where you stand.',
 array['SSC CGL','Beginners'], 'published', now() - interval '2 days')
on conflict (slug) do nothing;
